From: vyos-ls1046a-build CI <ci@local>
Date: Wed, 02 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fman: port: O/H frame injection + capture sink debugfs verbs (phase 1f)

Phase 1f of the O/H-port re-enqueue infra (T-M6-8,
plans/ASK2-VLAN-REARCH.md section 3, Option D). Phase 1e (0182,
board-validated 2026-09-02) proved a QMan FQ can be scheduled onto the
O/H port's own channel, but nothing has ever enqueued a frame into it
-- the port has never actually processed anything. This closes that
loop.

Two new verbs on the existing fman_port_oh_test debugfs tool:

- "capture_init" / "capture_fini" -- create/destroy ONE software-owned
  sink FQ (QMAN_FQ_FLAG_NO_ENQUEUE, scheduled on this CPU's own
  software portal channel via qman_affine_channel(), with a dqrr
  callback that logs each arrival's fqid/status/length/address and
  bumps a counter). Deliberately NOT reusing eth3/eth4's live kernel
  RX default FQID (0x200/0x300, per earlier F-197 board notes) as the
  observation point: dpaa_eth's RX handler expects every buffer
  arriving on ITS OWN FQs to carry ITS OWN private per-buffer
  bookkeeping (struct dpaa_eth_swbp etc.) at a fixed dpaa_bp-registered
  headroom -- handing it a foreign, hand-rolled buffer from this debug
  tool would have the RX handler misinterpret that region as driver
  state, a real corruption/crash risk on a live production interface.
  A fully separate, software-only sink FQ sidesteps that entirely:
  this file owns 100% of the interpretation of whatever lands in it.
  Teardown mirrors dpaa_eth.c's own dpaa_fq_free_entry() pattern
  (qman_retire_fq -> qman_oos_fq -> qman_destroy_fq -> release_fqid)
  for a scheduled consumer FQ -- phase 1e's unsched_fq() skips
  retire/oos because that FQ is QMAN_FQ_FLAG_TO_DCPORTAL (hardware
  consumer, not software-scheduled), a different class.

- "inject <hw_port_id>" -- build one minimal 64-byte frame (broadcast
  dst, dummy src, EtherType 0x0800; payload zeroed) in a kzalloc'd
  buffer, dma_map_single() it against the O/H port's own device
  (mirrors dpaa_eth.c's dpaa_tx() FD-building: qm_fd_clear_fd +
  fd.bpid = 0xff (FSL_DPAA_BPID_INV, "not BMan-pool-owned, don't
  auto-release") + qm_fd_set_contig + qm_fd_addr_set64), then
  qman_enqueue() it into that port's phase-1e input FQ. Requires
  sched_fq to have already run. The buffer is deliberately leaked (one
  64-byte kzalloc per call, never freed/unmapped): there is no
  completion signal back to this debug tool telling it when the O/H
  port hardware has actually finished reading the buffer, so freeing
  it here would be a genuine use-after-free -- an accepted, bounded
  cost for a manual one-frame-at-a-time silicon de-risk tool, not
  something a production path would ever do.

Whether the injected frame's parse succeeds (-> dflt_fqid) or takes a
parser gross-error (-> err_fqid) is not the point of this first test;
"bringup"'s existing target_fqid_hex argument (0180) should point BOTH
at the same capture_init sink so either outcome is observable.

Full board-test sequence for the next session (not yet run):
  capture_init                 # note the printed sink fqid
  bringup 4 <sink_fqid_hex>    # both dflt_fqid and err_fqid -> sink
  enable 4 1
  sched_fq 4
  inject 4
  # check dmesg for "oh_test capture: frame #1 ..."; confirm no wedge
  # (ping across eth3/eth4) throughout
  unsched_fq 4
  enable 4 0
  capture_fini

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate | Risk-Tier: A (new debug-only tool
extending 0177/0180/0182's existing verbs; the sink FQ is entirely
new and software-owned, the injected frame's buffer belongs to this
tool alone, gated behind manual debugfs writes, no automatic wiring,
RX/TX/existing TX FQ and phase-1e input-FQ paths untouched)
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c | 202 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 202 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
index 62e3b51..3e7d10f 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
@@ -14,6 +14,9 @@
 #include <linux/of_address.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/libfdt_env.h>
+#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
+#include <linux/atomic.h>
+#include <linux/smp.h>
 #include <soc/fsl/qman.h>
 #include <linux/debugfs.h>
 #include <linux/err.h>
@@ -2879,6 +2882,199 @@ static ssize_t fman_port_oh_test_unsched_fq(u8 port_id)
 	return 0;
 }

+/*
+ * ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1), phase 1f: close the loop on
+ * phase 1e's input FQ with an actual frame. Two new verbs:
+ *
+ * "capture_init" / "capture_fini" -- create/destroy ONE software-owned
+ * sink FQ (QMAN_FQ_FLAG_NO_ENQUEUE, scheduled on this CPU's own software
+ * portal channel via qman_affine_channel(), with a dqrr callback that
+ * just logs arrivals) completely decoupled from dpaa_eth's own RX FQs.
+ * Deliberately NOT reusing eth3/eth4's live kernel RX default FQID
+ * (0x200/0x300) as the observation point: dpaa_eth's RX handler expects
+ * every buffer arriving on its own FQs to carry its own private
+ * per-buffer bookkeeping (struct dpaa_eth_swbp etc.) at a fixed
+ * dpaa_bp-registered headroom -- handing it a foreign, hand-rolled
+ * buffer from this debug tool would misinterpret that region as driver
+ * state. A fully separate, software-only sink FQ has no such contract:
+ * this file owns 100% of the interpretation of whatever lands in it.
+ *
+ * "inject <hw_port_id>" -- build one minimal frame in a DMA-mapped
+ * buffer and qman_enqueue() it into that port's phase-1e input FQ.
+ * Requires sched_fq to have already run for the port. Does NOT require
+ * capture_init to have run first, but the injected frame's disposition
+ * (dflt_fqid/err_fqid, set by "bringup <port> [target_fqid_hex]") only
+ * lands somewhere observable if capture_init's printed sink fqid was
+ * passed as bringup's target_fqid_hex -- see this patch's commit
+ * message for the full board-test sequence.
+ */
+#define FMAN_PORT_OH_TEST_BPID_INV 0xff
+
+static struct {
+	struct qman_fq fq;
+	u32 fqid;
+	bool ready;
+	atomic_t rx_count;
+} fman_port_oh_capture;
+
+static enum qman_cb_dqrr_result
+fman_port_oh_capture_dqrr(struct qman_portal *portal, struct qman_fq *fq,
+			  const struct qm_dqrr_entry *dqrr, bool sched_napi)
+{
+	const struct qm_fd *fd = &dqrr->fd;
+	int count = atomic_inc_return(&fman_port_oh_capture.rx_count);
+
+	pr_info("fman_port: oh_test capture: frame #%d fqid=0x%x status=0x%x len=%d addr=0x%llx\n",
+		count, qman_fq_fqid(fq), be32_to_cpu(fd->status),
+		qm_fd_get_length(fd), (unsigned long long)qm_fd_addr(fd));
+	return qman_cb_dqrr_consume;
+}
+
+static ssize_t fman_port_oh_test_capture_init(void)
+{
+	struct qm_mcc_initfq initfq;
+	u32 fqid;
+	int err;
+
+	if (fman_port_oh_capture.ready)
+		return -EBUSY;
+
+	err = qman_alloc_fqid(&fqid);
+	if (err) {
+		pr_err("fman_port: oh_test capture_init: qman_alloc_fqid() = %d\n", err);
+		return err;
+	}
+
+	memset(&fman_port_oh_capture.fq, 0, sizeof(fman_port_oh_capture.fq));
+	fman_port_oh_capture.fq.fqid = fqid;
+	fman_port_oh_capture.fq.cb.dqrr = fman_port_oh_capture_dqrr;
+	err = qman_create_fq(fqid, QMAN_FQ_FLAG_NO_ENQUEUE, &fman_port_oh_capture.fq);
+	if (err) {
+		qman_release_fqid(fqid);
+		pr_err("fman_port: oh_test capture_init: qman_create_fq(0x%x) = %d\n",
+		       fqid, err);
+		return err;
+	}
+
+	memset(&initfq, 0, sizeof(initfq));
+	initfq.we_mask = cpu_to_be16(QM_INITFQ_WE_FQCTRL | QM_INITFQ_WE_DESTWQ);
+	initfq.fqd.fq_ctrl = cpu_to_be16(QM_FQCTRL_PREFERINCACHE);
+	qm_fqd_set_destwq(&initfq.fqd, qman_affine_channel(raw_smp_processor_id()), 0);
+	err = qman_init_fq(&fman_port_oh_capture.fq, QMAN_INITFQ_FLAG_SCHED, &initfq);
+	if (err) {
+		qman_destroy_fq(&fman_port_oh_capture.fq);
+		qman_release_fqid(fqid);
+		pr_err("fman_port: oh_test capture_init: qman_init_fq(0x%x) = %d\n",
+		       fqid, err);
+		return err;
+	}
+
+	fman_port_oh_capture.fqid = fqid;
+	atomic_set(&fman_port_oh_capture.rx_count, 0);
+	fman_port_oh_capture.ready = true;
+	pr_info("fman_port: oh_test capture_init OK, sink fqid=0x%x\n", fqid);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static ssize_t fman_port_oh_test_capture_fini(void)
+{
+	u32 fqid;
+	int err;
+
+	if (!fman_port_oh_capture.ready)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	fqid = fman_port_oh_capture.fqid;
+	err = qman_retire_fq(&fman_port_oh_capture.fq, NULL);
+	if (err < 0)
+		pr_err("fman_port: oh_test capture_fini: qman_retire_fq(0x%x) = %d\n",
+		       fqid, err);
+	err = qman_oos_fq(&fman_port_oh_capture.fq);
+	if (err < 0)
+		pr_err("fman_port: oh_test capture_fini: qman_oos_fq(0x%x) = %d\n",
+		       fqid, err);
+	qman_destroy_fq(&fman_port_oh_capture.fq);
+	qman_release_fqid(fqid);
+	fman_port_oh_capture.ready = false;
+	pr_info("fman_port: oh_test capture_fini: sink fqid=0x%x released, saw %d frame(s)\n",
+		fqid, atomic_read(&fman_port_oh_capture.rx_count));
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static ssize_t fman_port_oh_test_inject(u8 port_id)
+{
+	struct fman_port *port;
+	struct qm_fd fd;
+	dma_addr_t addr;
+	u8 *buf;
+	size_t len = 64;
+	int err;
+
+	if (port_id >= ARRAY_SIZE(fman_port_oh_input_fq))
+		return -EINVAL;
+	if (!fman_port_oh_input_fq[port_id].ready) {
+		pr_err("fman_port: oh_test inject 0x%02x: no input FQ (run sched_fq first)\n",
+		       port_id);
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	port = fman_port_oh_test_lookup(port_id, true);
+	if (!port)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	/*
+	 * Deliberately leaked: this is a one-shot debug tool with no
+	 * completion signal back to this function, so freeing (or
+	 * dma_unmap'ing) this buffer here -- before the O/H port hardware
+	 * has necessarily finished reading it -- would be a genuine
+	 * use-after-free. A 64-byte leak per "inject" call is the accepted
+	 * cost of a manual, one-frame-at-a-time silicon de-risk tool.
+	 */
+	buf = kzalloc(len, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!buf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	/*
+	 * Minimal Ethernet header so the hard parser has something to
+	 * chew on: broadcast dst, dummy locally-administered src,
+	 * EtherType 0x0800 (IPv4). Payload left zeroed. Whether this
+	 * parses clean (-> dflt_fqid) or takes a parser gross-error
+	 * (-> err_fqid) is not the point of this test -- "bringup"'s
+	 * target_fqid_hex should point BOTH at the same capture_init sink
+	 * so either outcome is observable.
+	 */
+	memset(buf, 0xff, 6);
+	buf[6] = 0x02;
+	buf[11] = 0x01;
+	buf[12] = 0x08;
+	buf[13] = 0x00;
+
+	addr = dma_map_single(port->dev, buf, len, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
+	if (dma_mapping_error(port->dev, addr)) {
+		kfree(buf);
+		pr_err("fman_port: oh_test inject 0x%02x: dma_map_single() failed\n",
+		       port_id);
+		return -EIO;
+	}
+
+	qm_fd_clear_fd(&fd);
+	fd.bpid = FMAN_PORT_OH_TEST_BPID_INV;
+	qm_fd_set_contig(&fd, 0, len);
+	qm_fd_addr_set64(&fd, addr);
+
+	err = qman_enqueue(&fman_port_oh_input_fq[port_id].fq, &fd);
+	if (err) {
+		pr_err("fman_port: oh_test inject 0x%02x: qman_enqueue() = %d\n",
+		       port_id, err);
+		return err;
+	}
+
+	pr_info("fman_port: oh_test inject 0x%02x OK, input fqid=0x%x addr=0x%llx len=%zu (watch dmesg for oh_test capture)\n",
+		port_id, fman_port_oh_input_fq[port_id].fqid,
+		(unsigned long long)addr, len);
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static ssize_t fman_port_oh_test_write(struct file *file,
 				       const char __user *buf,
 				       size_t count, loff_t *ppos)
@@ -2903,6 +3099,12 @@ static ssize_t fman_port_oh_test_write(struct file *file,
 		err = fman_port_oh_test_sched_fq(port_id);
 	else if (sscanf(kbuf, "unsched_fq %hhu", &port_id) == 1)
 		err = fman_port_oh_test_unsched_fq(port_id);
+	else if (strcmp(kbuf, "capture_init") == 0)
+		err = fman_port_oh_test_capture_init();
+	else if (strcmp(kbuf, "capture_fini") == 0)
+		err = fman_port_oh_test_capture_fini();
+	else if (sscanf(kbuf, "inject %hhu", &port_id) == 1)
+		err = fman_port_oh_test_inject(port_id);
 	else
 		return -EINVAL;

